---
// The homepage hero's scroll-scrubbed transformation: the salon's own four dogs are groomed —
// desaturated to full colour behind a soft right-to-left reveal front — as the Fairy Tails
// fairy showers them with stars. Ported from the design handoff v2
// (`Luxury dog grooming animation/design_handoff_hero_animation/`); read its README for the
// choreography. Its easing, timing windows and particle maths are FINAL and reproduced verbatim.
//
// ⚠️ v2 REPLACED v1 (2026-08-09). v1 was a single scruffy puppy with a bow-pop payoff. The bow,
// the 6-star burst and the ground-shadow ellipse are GONE — they exist in neither the new
// handoff nor the new artwork. Do not resurrect them from git history.
//
// The deliberate divergences from the handoff, and why:
//
// 1. COLOUR. The handoff is designed against the salon's OLD brand blue (#00AFF1 — the legacy
//    "K9 Centre" logo) on a #FFFFFF page. This site is "Countryside editorial" (global.css
//    @theme): cream paper, moss greens, honey accents, and NO blue anywhere. Every blue literal
//    is remapped onto honey. The remap also re-anchors LIGHTNESS: the blue set carried its
//    sparkle on two dark members (#00AFF1, #0090C8) that only work on white — against this
//    hero's moss-900 band they are the weakest of the four. Corollary: honey-500/600/700 must
//    never be used for sparkle here — on moss they read as mud.
//
// 2. THE FAIRY IS PAINTED BY CSS. Her artwork's RGB is a flat blue gradient (hue locked
//    196–197°, S 100%, L 47%→30% top-to-bottom) — there is no modelling in it worth keeping,
//    only her alpha. Worse, that gradient runs light-at-head/dark-at-skirt: correct on white
//    (contrast grows downward 2.58→5.77), backwards on moss (4.74→2.12 — her skirt sinks into
//    the band), and no filter can invert a gradient's direction. So scripts/hero-assets.mjs
//    reduces her to an alpha stencil and CSS masks a honey gradient through it. Her artwork is
//    UNCHANGED between handoff v1 and v2, so the stencil is a straight drop-in (both 440×578).
//
// 3. NO JS LAYOUT. The handoff scales the stage by measuring the container in JS and writing px
//    onto it. Safe there — its script is inline and synchronous — but Astro hoists component
//    scripts to a DEFERRED type="module", so ported as-is it would size the stage after first
//    paint: layout shift on every load. Here the whole static composition is percentages of an
//    `aspect-ratio: 760/620` box, laid out and scaled entirely by CSS: zero CLS, and no element
//    is ever sized in px, so nothing can overflow a narrow viewport. Only the PARTICLES — which
//    exist solely because JS is running — live in `.ft-fx`, a 760×620 layer scaled by a JS-set
//    `--ft-s` (from the CONTAINER's width, never innerWidth) defaulting to scale(0).
//    ⚠️ index.astro's full-bleed hero DEPENDS on `.ft-stage { width:100%; aspect-ratio:760/620 }`
//    staying exactly that — the wrapper carries the sizing and the stage fills it. Switching this
//    to `height:100%` or adding `max-width` breaks the hero layout.
//
// 4. AUTHORED FINISHED, ARMED BACKWARDS. The markup renders the FINAL state — groomed pack in
//    full colour, fairy present. The p=0 "before" state is imposed by CSS, only under
//    [data-ft-armed] and only inside @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference). This is the
//    inverse of the handoff (which bakes p=0 into its HTML) and it buys two hard requirements
//    for free: with JS off the hero is a correct finished photograph rather than a grey pack
//    with no fairy, and reduced-motion users get the finished state with no flash.
//
// 5. TWO MODES, ONE TIMELINE. Desktop (≥lg) scrubs a sticky 240vh track, per the handoff (whose
//    340vh is a documented tuning knob). Below lg there is NO scroll-jack: the stage plays
//    through once when it scrolls into view. Owner rule (CLAUDE.md): most of this salon's
//    customers arrive on a phone, and pinning them for 3.4 screens before any content is a
//    conversion cost the animation has not earned. Only the PRODUCER of p forks; update(p) is
//    one pure function, not two code paths.
import { getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import dogsSrc from '../assets/pages/home/hero-dogs.webp';
import dogsShadowSrc from '../assets/pages/home/hero-dogs-shadow.webp';
import fairyStencil from '../assets/pages/home/hero-fairy-stencil.png';

// The pack and its shadow are PRE-ENCODED webp by scripts/hero-assets.mjs and imported directly
// rather than run through getImage(). Astro's sharp service does not expose `alphaQuality`, and
// alphaQuality 75 is visually lossless on this near-binary rim (max |Δα| = 2/255) while saving
// 22KB — see that script's header for the full trade. An imported .webp is emitted to _astro/
// byte-identical (verified against a real build), so `.src` is a stable, deterministic URL.
//
// That single URL matters as much here as it did for v1: the pack is rendered TWICE (muted base
// + full-colour reveal layer) AND used as the sheen's CSS mask. One URL = one fetch. Never add
// widths/densities — the mask can only name one candidate, so a srcset would very likely name
// the one the browser didn't pick and pay for a second download.
const dogs = dogsSrc;
const dogsShadow = dogsShadowSrc;

// The fairy's mask, at native width: downscaling a hard-edged two-tone stencil ADDS antialiasing
// entropy (440w webp 8.4KB · 360w 19.3KB · 300w 15.5KB). webp beats her PNG (8.4 vs 12.8KB)
// because hero-assets.mjs flattens her RGB to a constant, leaving only alpha to encode. Verified
// to decode as a mask in WebKit — see scripts/hero-mask-support.mjs, which exists because a mask
// that fails to load falls back to `none`, and an unmasked fairy is a solid gold RECTANGLE.
const fairy = await getImage({ src: fairyStencil, format: 'webp', width: 440 });

// Stage coordinates from the handoff live in a 760×620 space; percentages keep that exact
// composition while letting CSS do the scaling. Kept as literal maths so the handoff's numbers
// stay greppable against its README.
const SW = 760;
const SH = 620;
const pct = (n: number, of: number) => `${((n / of) * 100).toFixed(4)}%`;
---

<div class="ft-stage" data-hero-stage aria-hidden="true">
  {/* Ambient motes — the only time-based (non-scroll) motion in the piece. Alphas are lifted
      ~+0.06 over the handoff's: at 5–8px under blur(1px), its values composite to roughly
      (102,97,62) over moss — present, but too timid to register. */}
  <div class="ft-mote ft-mote-1"></div>
  <div class="ft-mote ft-mote-2"></div>
  <div class="ft-mote ft-mote-3"></div>

  {/* Contact shadow. The handoff says "no ground shadow div — the pack photo carries its own
      natural shadows", and on its WHITE page that is true. On moss-900 it is false: 57.1% of
      that baked field composites LIGHTER than the band (mean +19.9 L), i.e. it reads as dirt,
      not contact. scripts/hero-assets.mjs therefore cuts it out of the photograph and re-emits
      it as this alpha-only stencil, which CSS paints in a colour DARKER than the band in every
      channel. Same idiom as the fairy: keep the alpha, throw the studio's grey away.

      It is a SIBLING of .ft-dogs, not a child, and that is deliberate — during the proud lift
      the pack rises but its contact shadow must not rise with it. A real shadow tightens and
      fades instead, which update() does with a scale and an opacity. */}
  <div class="ft-dogs-shadow" data-dogs-shadow style={`--ft-shadow-mask: url('${dogsShadow.src}')`}>
  </div>

  {/* The pack: muted "before" underneath, full-colour "after" on top behind a soft reveal mask.
      Plain <img>, not <Image>: we need the single shared URL (see the import note above), and
      width/height are set so the box is reserved before the bytes arrive.

      loading="lazy" is deliberate and does the right thing on BOTH layouts, because lazy only
      defers images that are OUT of the viewport: on desktop the stage fills the hero band, so it
      loads at once; on a phone it sits below the fold, so it waits. That matters more than it
      sounds. The LCP element here is the H1, which cannot paint until Fraunces arrives — and an
      eager, fetchpriority="high" pack queues 45KB of decoration AHEAD of the font the headline
      is waiting on. The photograph must never outrank the headline. */}
  <div class="ft-dogs" data-dogs>
    <img
      class="ft-dogs-img ft-dogs-before"
      src={dogs.src}
      width={dogs.width}
      height={dogs.height}
      alt=""
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
      fetchpriority="low"
      data-before
    />
    <div class="ft-after" data-after>
      <img
        class="ft-dogs-img"
        src={dogs.src}
        width={dogs.width}
        height={dogs.height}
        alt=""
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        fetchpriority="low"
      />
      {/* The mask URL must be interpolated into an INLINE STYLE attribute, never written as a
          literal url() in the <style> block below: a scoped block is compiled statically, so it
          cannot see this runtime value, and a literal path there would be resolved by Vite
          against the wrong thing — silently 404ing with no build warning and no gate to catch
          it. This is the one hazard in the whole component that nothing downstream would flag. */}
      <div class="ft-sheen-clip" style={`--ft-dogs-mask: url('${dogs.src}')`}>
        <div class="ft-sheen" data-sheen></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* Fairy — static by explicit client request (no path movement); she only fades and settles.
      The glow lives on this wrapper, NOT on .ft-fairy-art: CSS applies filter BEFORE mask, so a
      drop-shadow on the masked element would be a shadow of its rectangular box which its own
      mask then clips away. On the parent it correctly traces her silhouette. */}
  <div class="ft-fairy" data-fairy>
    <div class="ft-fairy-art" style={`--ft-fairy-mask: url('${fairy.src}')`}></div>
    <svg class="ft-wand-star" viewBox="-6 -6 12 12" width="22" height="22">
      <path
        d="M0 -5 C0.9 -1.6 1.6 -0.9 5 0 C1.6 0.9 0.9 1.6 0 5 C-0.9 1.6 -1.6 0.9 -5 0 C-1.6 -0.9 -0.9 -1.6 0 -5 Z"
        fill="#fbf7ed"></path>
    </svg>
  </div>

  {/* Particle layer — populated by JS only. scale(0) until measured, so nothing can flash. */}
  <div class="ft-fx" data-fx></div>
</div>

<script is:inline>
  // Arms the stage to its p=0 "before" state BEFORE first paint. The markup is authored at the
  // FINISHED state (see divergence 4), and the module script that drives the animation is
  // deferred — so without this, every load would paint the groomed pack and then snap backwards.
  //
  // The safety net matters as much as the arming: if the module never initialises (bundle
  // failed, threw, browser too old), disarm and let the finished photograph stand. The brief
  // requires the hero stay usable when the animation does not load, and a permanently grey pack
  // with no fairy is not that. JS off entirely → this never runs → finished state → also right.
  (function () {
    var s = document.querySelector('[data-hero-stage]');
    if (!s) return;
    s.setAttribute('data-ft-armed', '');
    setTimeout(function () {
      if (!s.dataset.ftInit) s.removeAttribute('data-ft-armed');
    }, 3000);
  })();
</script>

<style
  define:vars={{
    dogsL: pct(30, SW),
    dogsT: pct(256, SH),
    dogsW: pct(640, SW),
    dogsH: pct(330, SH),
    fairyL: pct(580, SW),
    fairyT: pct(10, SH),
    fairyW: pct(170, SW),
    fairyH: pct(223, SH),
  }}
>
  /* The stage is a pure-CSS box: aspect-ratio reserves it before JS, and every child is a
     percentage of it, so the composition scales to any width with no measuring and no shift. */
  .ft-stage {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 760 / 620;
    /* The stage never eats a tap, a scroll or a drag — the brief's hard rule that no
       interaction may interfere with scrolling, navigation or buttons. Now that the stage is
       full-bleed it covers the whole hero band, so this is load-bearing, not a nicety. */
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .ft-mote {
    position: absolute;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(1px);
    animation: ftFloat 6s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
  }
  .ft-mote-1 {
    left: 15.7895%;
    top: 19.3548%;
    width: 1.0526%;
    background: rgb(243 220 174 / 0.42);
  }
  .ft-mote-2 {
    left: 92.1053%;
    top: 54.8387%;
    width: 0.7895%;
    background: rgb(236 194 124 / 0.34);
    animation-duration: 7.5s;
    animation-delay: 0.8s;
  }
  .ft-mote-3 {
    left: 11.8421%;
    top: 69.3548%;
    width: 0.6579%;
    background: rgb(236 194 124 / 0.26);
    animation-duration: 5.2s;
    animation-delay: 1.6s;
  }
  @keyframes ftFloat {
    0% {
      transform: translateY(0);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translateY(-14px);
    }
  }

  /* The contact shadow occupies the SAME box as the pack — the stencil was rendered from the
     photograph's own field, so it is already registered to it. Under this box the painted area
     lands at stage x 38..654, y 459..574: directly under the paws. */
  .ft-dogs-shadow {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--dogsL);
    top: var(--dogsT);
    width: var(--dogsW);
    height: var(--dogsH);
    z-index: 2;
    transform-origin: 50% 100%;
    /* #141c0d is darker than moss-900 (#2c3823) in every channel. On a dark band the only thing
       that reads as contact is a core DARKER than the band — the grey the studio baked in was
       LIGHTER, which is exactly why it had to be re-rendered rather than kept. */
    background: #141c0d;
    opacity: 0.85;
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--ft-shadow-mask);
    -webkit-mask-size: 100% 100%;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-image: var(--ft-shadow-mask);
    mask-size: 100% 100%;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  .ft-dogs {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--dogsL);
    top: var(--dogsT);
    width: var(--dogsW);
    height: var(--dogsH);
    z-index: 3;
    will-change: transform;
    /* FINISHED state = the proud lift, held. Must match update(1) exactly, or JS-off and
       reduced-motion render the pack 6px lower than the animation's own end state. */
    transform: translateY(-6px);
  }
  .ft-dogs-img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }
  /* FINISHED state: the muted layer is gone and the after-layer is fully revealed. */
  .ft-dogs-before {
    opacity: 0;
    /* The handoff's saturate(.4) brightness(.94) was tuned against a WHITE page, where merely
       draining the colour is enough to read as dull. On a dark band a desaturated warm subject
       still reads as lit and healthy, so the brightness drop is deepened until the pack looks
       genuinely unkempt — but kept warm rather than grey: a scruffy dog is dusty, not monochrome. */
    filter: saturate(0.34) brightness(0.72) contrast(0.94);
  }
  .ft-after {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }
  .ft-sheen-clip {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* Hand-prefixed: this repo has no autoprefixer (postcss.config.mjs loads only
       @tailwindcss/postcss), matching global.css's .paw-bullet. */
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--ft-dogs-mask);
    -webkit-mask-size: 100% 100%;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-image: var(--ft-dogs-mask);
    mask-size: 100% 100%;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
  }
  .ft-sheen {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: -20%;
    width: 30%;
    height: 140%;
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateX(-120%);
    background: linear-gradient(
      102deg,
      rgb(251 247 237 / 0) 0%,
      rgb(251 247 237 / 0.32) 50%,
      rgb(251 247 237 / 0) 100%
    );
  }

  .ft-fairy {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--fairyL);
    top: var(--fairyT);
    width: var(--fairyW);
    height: var(--fairyH);
    z-index: 6;
    transform-origin: 50% 60%;
    /* On a dark band a drop shadow only muddies her; she is the light SOURCE the scene is lit
       by, so she glows instead. Must live here, not on .ft-fairy-art — see the markup note. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 16px rgb(236 194 124 / 0.3));
  }
  .ft-fairy-art {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    -webkit-mask: var(--ft-fairy-mask) 0 0 / 100% 100% no-repeat;
    mask: var(--ft-fairy-mask) 0 0 / 100% 100% no-repeat;
    /* Her colour, in one line. Runs light-at-head → gold-at-hem: it keeps the artwork's
       light-from-above reading, but compressed so the darkest stop still holds 4.5:1 against
       the band instead of the original's 2.12. */
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, #f3dcae 0%, #ecc27c 38%, #dfa854 72%, #cf9038 100%);
  }
  .ft-wand-star {
    position: absolute;
    /* Centred on the WAND TIP, verified against the artwork's own alpha: these fractions map to
       fairy-color.png pixel (27,230), alpha 255, inside an isolated lobe (bbox x 4..60,
       y 211..274, centroid 31.3,236.4) that is unmistakably the 4-point star on the end of her
       wand. The handoff's own two coordinates — the visible star at wrapper (17,119) and the
       emission origin at stage (606,138) — BOTH land on alpha 0, in the transparent notch below
       her arm, 21px and 25px from any opaque pixel. So this correction applies to TIP in the
       script as well, not just to this star. */
    left: 6.1111%;
    top: 39.8305%;
    width: 12.2222%;
    height: auto;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  }

  .ft-fx {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0;
    width: 760px;
    height: 620px;
    z-index: 5;
    transform-origin: top left;
    /* scale(0) until JS measures: particles must never flash at 1:1 on a small stage. */
    transform: scale(var(--ft-s, 0));
  }

  /* ── The p=0 "before" state ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Imposed only when JS has armed the stage AND the visitor accepts motion. Everything the
     animation drives must have its start value here, or it will pop on the first frame. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    [data-ft-armed] .ft-dogs-before {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    [data-ft-armed] .ft-dogs {
      transform: none; /* the pack has not earned the lift yet */
    }
    [data-ft-armed] .ft-dogs-shadow {
      transform: none;
      opacity: 0.85;
    }
    /* ⚠️ These two stops are not decorative and not a round number chosen by eye. The reveal
       front is retuned (see `front()` in the script) precisely so that this string is what
       update(0) writes: 100%/118% is the SMALLEST pair that still hides the whole 640-wide box
       while preserving the handoff's 18-percentage-point ramp width, so the CSS slope matches
       the JS slope and nothing pops on the first frame. Change `front()`'s endpoints and you
       must recompute these two numbers with it. */
    [data-ft-armed] .ft-after {
      -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 100%, #000 118%);
      mask-image: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent 100%, #000 118%);
    }
    [data-ft-armed] .ft-fairy {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: scale(0.96);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .ft-mote {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Drives the stage. One pure update(p); only the PRODUCER of p forks by viewport.
  //
  // ⚠️ Astro dedupes component scripts: this module runs at most ONCE per session, even across
  // client-side navigations (ClientRouter). Two consequences shape everything below:
  //   · Document-scoped listeners are registered HERE, at module scope — never inside init(),
  //     where they would accumulate one per navigation (the stage element is recreated on every
  //     swap; nothing in src/ uses transition:persist).
  //   · init() must re-run per page via astro:page-load, or navigating back to / would leave a
  //     fresh, never-initialised stage: permanently grey, unscrubbed, un-armed. The failure mode
  //     of getting this wrong is NOT double-init — the dedupe makes that impossible — it is
  //     nothing running at all.
  const STAR =
    'M0 -5 C0.9 -1.6 1.6 -0.9 5 0 C1.6 0.9 0.9 1.6 0 5 C-0.9 1.6 -1.6 0.9 -5 0 C-1.6 -0.9 -0.9 -1.6 0 -5 Z';

  // ── Tuning knobs (the handoff names track length and dust count as the intended two; the
  // mobile playthrough's duration is the third, and is ours). ────────────────────────────────
  const N_DUST = 34;
  const PLAY_MS = 3400;

  // The honey remap of the handoff's blue palette — see divergence 1 in the frontmatter.
  const DUST_COLORS = ['#ecc27c', '#f3dcae', '#dfa854', '#fbf7ed'];

  // ── Stage constants (760×620 space) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Emission origin = the fairy's WAND TIP, verified against her artwork's alpha. The handoff's
  // own (606,138) lands on a fully transparent pixel 25px from anything opaque — see the
  // .ft-wand-star note in the style block. This is the same fraction the visible star uses, so
  // the stars provably come out of the wand rather than out of her skirt.
  const TIP = [590, 99];
  const DOG_L = 30;
  const DOG_W = 640;

  // The pack's top outline, right → left. The handoff's 11 anchors are CORRECT — measured
  // against the cleaned artwork's alpha they sit 1.2–6.1px clear ABOVE the fur (mean +2.3), a
  // deliberate hair's-breadth clearance — so they are kept verbatim. Two anchors are ADDED to
  // fix two genuine defects, and the left end is extended 69 → 64 to reach the art:
  //   · [662,332] — without it contourY clamps flat at y=277 for x ≥ 628 while the right-hand
  //     dog's shoulder actually falls away to y=334, leaving stars hanging up to 79px in mid-air.
  //   · [206,336] — the chord (245,315)→(186,391) cut up to 26.9px INSIDE the third dog's back,
  //     burying stars in the fur.
  // ⚠️ The straight segments between anchors are a deliberate ENVELOPE: they bridge the
  // inter-dog notches (x≈190–198, 332–344, 470–488, where the true "top" drops to leg level at
  // y 503–512) rather than diving into them. Do NOT replace this with a per-column measured top
  // — the stars would rain into the gaps between the dogs.
  // ⚠️ The LEFT-HAND anchors (x ≤ 206) were re-measured on 2026-08-09 when the handoff's cropped
  // terrier was replaced by the owner's beagle (see scripts/hero-assets.mjs). The beagle stands
  // ~47px taller, so the handoff's [127,373] ended up 47px INSIDE its head and [186,391] / [64,379]
  // landed on empty stage. Anything that changes the artwork changes these numbers — re-measure,
  // never re-guess. Current set verified at a mean 3.6px clearance above the fur.
  const CONTOUR: [number, number][] = [
    [662, 332],
    [628, 277],
    [569, 257],
    [510, 303],
    [452, 313],
    [392, 282],
    [363, 318],
    [304, 321],
    [245, 315],
    [206, 336],
    [160, 337],
    [130, 323],
    [100, 326],
    [80, 345],
    [68, 356],
  ];

  // The handoff's own explicit 9 — NOT derived from CONTOUR (v1 did that because its artwork had
  // no separate list). All nine verified against the artwork; x=98 sits 0.5px above the fur.
  // The two leftmost moved with the beagle swap: [186,391] now sits in the empty notch between
  // the beagle and the staffie (it would float in mid-air), and [98,373] was 47px below the
  // beagle's head. Both re-measured; each now sits 2–4px clear above the fur.
  const GLINTS: [number, number][] = [
    [628, 277],
    [569, 257],
    [510, 303],
    [452, 313],
    [392, 282],
    [304, 321],
    [245, 315],
    [160, 337],
    [98, 326],
  ];

  const S = (p: number, a: number, b: number) => Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (p - a) / (b - a)));
  const eio = (t: number) => (t < 0.5 ? 4 * t * t * t : 1 - Math.pow(-2 * t + 2, 3) / 2);
  const eo = (t: number) => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3);
  const eioInv = (v: number) =>
    v <= 0 ? 0 : v >= 1 ? 1 : v < 0.5 ? Math.cbrt(v / 4) : 1 - Math.cbrt(2 * (1 - v)) / 2;

  // The reveal front, retuned from the handoff's 690 → 10. Its sweep must clear the box at BOTH
  // ends once the mask's ±9% soft edge is accounted for, and the handoff's does not: at p=0 its
  // ramp starts at stage x 632 while the pack runs to 667, so a 34px wedge of the right-hand dog
  // is ALREADY in colour on frame one (alpha 0.298 at the art's edge) — over a fully-opaque
  // muted layer, i.e. a visible partial-colour smear before the visitor has scrolled.
  // Solving for the endpoints that actually clear a 640-wide box with a ±9% edge:
  //     hide all at p=0     → e ≥ 109  → frontX ≥ 30 + 109×6.4 = 727.6
  //     reveal all at p=.70 → e ≤ −9   → frontX ≤ 30 −   9×6.4 = −27.6
  // Only the two endpoint scalars change; the 0.14–0.70 window, the easing and the ±9% edge are
  // the handoff's, untouched. This is also what makes the armed CSS exactly 100%/118%.
  const front = (p: number) => 728 - 756 * eio(S(p, 0.14, 0.7));

  // ⚠️ The SHOWER gets its own front, anchored to the artwork's measured extents (60.6…666.7,
  // inset ~3px). Reusing `front()` for landing points is what the handoff does, and because that
  // curve deliberately overshoots the box at both ends to serve the mask's soft edge, 27 of the
  // 34 stars land off the pack — ten of them to the LEFT of the dog box entirely (one at
  // negative stage x), which is the last third of the shower, i.e. the payoff, playing out in
  // empty moss beside the dogs. Re-anchoring drops that to 15, of which 12 are within 9px of the
  // fur — the intended `dy` scatter.
  // ⚠️ A two-sided CLAMP on the handoff's front() also removes every out-of-bounds landing but
  // piles 10 of the 34 stars on exactly x = 64. Use the remap, not the clamp.
  // Re-measured after the beagle swap: the pack's painted extent is now stage x 66.6..666.7
  // (it was 60.6 on the left with the terrier), inset ~5px so stars land ON fur, not on its rim.
  const ART_L = 72;
  const ART_R = 662;
  const pfront = (p: number) => ART_R - (ART_R - ART_L) * eio(S(p, 0.14, 0.7));

  function contourY(x: number) {
    if (x >= CONTOUR[0][0]) return CONTOUR[0][1];
    for (let i = 0; i < CONTOUR.length - 1; i++) {
      const a = CONTOUR[i];
      const b = CONTOUR[i + 1];
      if (x <= a[0] && x >= b[0]) return a[1] + ((b[1] - a[1]) * (a[0] - x)) / (a[0] - b[0] || 1);
    }
    return CONTOUR[CONTOUR.length - 1][1];
  }

  let teardown: (() => void) | null = null;

  function initHeroStage() {
    const stage = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hero-stage]');
    const track = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-hero-track]');
    if (!stage || !track || stage.dataset.ftInit) return;
    stage.dataset.ftInit = '1';

    const q = <T extends Element>(s: string) => stage.querySelector<T>(s);
    const fx = q<HTMLElement>('[data-fx]')!;
    const elDogs = q<HTMLElement>('[data-dogs]')!;
    const elShadow = q<HTMLElement>('[data-dogs-shadow]')!;
    const elBefore = q<HTMLElement>('[data-before]')!;
    const elAfter = q<HTMLElement>('[data-after]')!;
    const elSheen = q<HTMLElement>('[data-sheen]')!;
    const elFairy = q<HTMLElement>('[data-fairy]')!;

    // ── Particles. One fragment, one reflow. No will-change: 43 permanently-promoted
    // compositor layers costs more in raster memory on a mid-range phone than it saves. ──────
    const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
    const mkStar = (size: number, color: string, glow: boolean) => {
      const d = document.createElement('div');
      d.style.cssText = 'position:absolute;left:0;top:0;opacity:0';
      d.innerHTML =
        `<svg width="${size}" height="${size}" viewBox="-6 -6 12 12"` +
        (glow ? ' style="filter:drop-shadow(0 0 4px rgba(243,220,174,.75))"' : '') +
        `><path d="${STAR}" fill="${color}"></path></svg>`;
      frag.appendChild(d);
      return d;
    };

    // Sizes are the handoff's AUTHORED values, deliberately un-compensated. v1 sized its burst
    // UP because the old two-column stage rendered at --ft-s ≈ 0.78 (a 594px stage). Full-bleed
    // puts --ft-s at 1.0–1.9 across desktop, i.e. at or above the s=1 the handoff authors for,
    // so any such compensation must be REMOVED, not carried over. If the sparkle ever reads too
    // heavy on a very large monitor, clamp --ft-s rather than re-tuning per-element sizes.
    const dust = Array.from({ length: N_DUST }, (_, i) => {
      const s = (i * 0.618034) % 1;
      const s2 = (i * 0.377) % 1;
      return {
        el: mkStar(10 + Math.round(s2 * 9), DUST_COLORS[i % 4], true),
        t: 0.1 + (i / (N_DUST - 1)) * 0.52 + (s - 0.5) * 0.02,
        dx: (s2 - 0.5) * 44,
        dy: -10 + s * 26,
        arc: s2 * 46,
        rot: s * 360,
      };
    });

    const glints = GLINTS.map((pt, i) => {
      // Pop exactly as the reveal front crosses their x. eioInv is the analytic inverse of the
      // front's easing, so this stays correct without simulating the timeline.
      const v = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (728 - pt[0]) / 756));
      const el = mkStar(i % 2 ? 19 : 26, i % 3 === 2 ? '#fbf7ed' : '#f3dcae', false);
      el.style.left = `${pt[0]}px`;
      el.style.top = `${pt[1] - 14}px`;
      el.style.zIndex = '4';
      return { el, t: 0.14 + 0.56 * eioInv(v) };
    });
    fx.appendChild(frag);

    // ── The whole animation: one pure function of p ───────────────────────────────────────
    function update(p: number) {
      const frontX = front(p);
      const fo = S(p, 0.04, 0.1);
      elFairy.style.opacity = String(fo);
      elFairy.style.transform = `scale(${0.96 + 0.04 * eo(fo)})`;

      // e = the reveal edge as a percentage of the 640-wide box (640/100 = 6.4 stage px per 1%).
      const e = (frontX - DOG_L) / (DOG_W / 100);
      const g = `linear-gradient(90deg, rgba(0,0,0,0) ${e - 9}%, #000 ${e + 9}%)`;
      elAfter.style.setProperty('-webkit-mask-image', g);
      elAfter.style.setProperty('mask-image', g);
      elBefore.style.opacity = String(1 - S(p, 0.66, 0.73));

      const liftT = eo(S(p, 0.74, 0.84));
      elDogs.style.transform = `translateY(${-6 * liftT}px)`;
      // A contact shadow does not rise with the subject — it tightens and fades. Anchored at
      // transform-origin 50% 100%, so it contracts toward the paws rather than drifting.
      elShadow.style.transform = `scale(${1 - 0.03 * liftT})`;
      elShadow.style.opacity = String(0.85 * (1 - 0.1 * liftT));

      const sp = S(p, 0.78, 0.93);
      elSheen.style.transform = `translateX(${-120 + 480 * eio(sp)}%)`;
      elSheen.style.opacity = sp > 0 && sp < 1 ? '1' : '0';

      for (const s of glints) {
        const lo = S(p, s.t - 0.035, s.t + 0.05);
        s.el.style.transform = `translate(-50%,-50%) scale(${0.4 + 0.8 * eo(lo)}) rotate(${lo * 90}deg)`;
        s.el.style.opacity = lo <= 0 || lo >= 1 ? '0' : String(Math.sin(lo * Math.PI));
      }

      for (const d of dust) {
        const a = (p - d.t) / 0.11;
        if (a <= 0 || a >= 1) {
          d.el.style.opacity = '0';
          continue;
        }
        const lx = Math.min(ART_R, Math.max(ART_L, pfront(d.t + 0.06) + d.dx));
        const ly = contourY(lx) + d.dy;
        const cx = (TIP[0] + lx) / 2;
        const cy = Math.min(TIP[1], ly) - 60 - d.arc;
        const u = 1 - a;
        const x = u * u * TIP[0] + 2 * u * a * cx + a * a * lx;
        const y = u * u * TIP[1] + 2 * u * a * cy + a * a * ly;
        d.el.style.transform = `translate(${x}px,${y}px) scale(${0.5 + 0.6 * a}) rotate(${d.rot + a * 60}deg)`;
        d.el.style.opacity = String(Math.min(1, a * 5) * (a > 0.8 ? (1 - a) / 0.2 : 1) * 0.95);
      }
    }

    // ── Scale the particle layer from the CONTAINER's width — never innerWidth. A stage that
    // renders unscaled at 760px is the one way this component can overflow a phone. ──────────
    const ro = new ResizeObserver(([entry]) => {
      const w = entry.contentRect.width;
      if (!w) return; // zero-width (display:none ancestor) would set scale(0) and hide it silently
      fx.style.setProperty('--ft-s', String(w / 760));
    });
    ro.observe(stage);

    // ── Producers of p ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    const mqDesktop = matchMedia('(min-width: 64rem)'); // rem, to track Tailwind's lg exactly
    const mqReduce = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

    let p = 0;
    let rendered = -1;
    let raf = 0;
    let playT0 = 0;
    let played = false;
    let visible = false;
    // Has the STAGE itself actually been seen? Distinct from `visible`, which tracks the TRACK
    // (with a 200px head start) purely to decide whether the loop is worth running. Conflating
    // the two is a real bug, not a theoretical one — see the armer below.
    let playArmed = false;
    let mode: 'scroll' | 'play' | 'static' = 'static';

    const render = (v: number) => {
      if (v === rendered) return; // parked at a stable p: skip the writes, keep the loop cheap
      rendered = v;
      update(v);
    };

    function scrollTarget() {
      const r = track.getBoundingClientRect();
      const span = r.height - innerHeight;
      return span > 10 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, -r.top / span)) : r.top < 0 ? 1 : 0;
    }

    function frame(now: number) {
      raf = 0;
      if (mode === 'scroll') {
        const t = scrollTarget();
        const d = t - p;
        p = Math.abs(d) < 0.0005 ? t : p + d * 0.16; // damped approach = the engineered scrub feel
        render(p);
        if (visible) raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
      } else if (mode === 'play') {
        p = Math.min(1, (now - playT0) / PLAY_MS);
        render(p);
        if (p >= 1) played = true;
        else if (visible) raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
      }
    }

    const start = () => {
      if (raf || mode === 'static') return;
      // In play mode the clock must not tick until the stage has actually been seen.
      if (mode === 'play' && !playArmed && !played) return;
      raf = requestAnimationFrame(frame);
    };
    const stop = () => {
      if (raf) cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
      raf = 0;
    };

    function applyMode() {
      stop();
      if (mqReduce.matches) {
        // Not "less motion" but a different render path: the finished state, no loop, no
        // particles moving. The CSS arming is already inert under this media query, so the
        // markup is correct on its own — update(1) only pins it against a live mode switch.
        mode = 'static';
        p = 1;
        render(1);
        return;
      }
      if (mqDesktop.matches) {
        mode = 'scroll';
        p = scrollTarget(); // snap, don't damp: new geometry, same tick, no lurch
        render(p);
      } else {
        mode = 'play';
        if (played || p >= 1) {
          p = 1;
          render(1);
          return; // never replay on a rotation
        }
        // A scrub abandoned mid-way continues by time from exactly where it stopped — but only
        // once the stage has been seen. Seeding the clock before that is what burns it unseen.
        if (playArmed) playT0 = performance.now() - p * PLAY_MS;
      }
      if (visible) start();
    }

    // Off-screen the loop is pure battery burn. rootMargin gives it a head start so it is
    // already settled by the time the stage is on screen.
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => {
        visible = entry.isIntersecting;
        if (visible) {
          if (mode === 'play' && !played && playArmed) playT0 = performance.now() - p * PLAY_MS;
          start();
        } else {
          stop();
          // Snap to where the scroll says we are. Without this, flicking past the track leaves
          // the hero frozen half-groomed for the rest of the session.
          if (mode === 'scroll') {
            p = scrollTarget();
            render(p);
          }
        }
      },
      { rootMargin: '200px 0px' },
    );
    io.observe(track);

    // Arms the play-once clock, and it MUST watch the stage rather than the track.
    //
    // Below lg the `lg:h-[240vh]` collapses, so the track IS the whole hero — it starts at the
    // top of the document and is therefore already intersecting at scrollY=0, 200px head start
    // or not. Seeding the clock from that observer meant the 3.4s playthrough ran to completion
    // while the stage was still below the fold, latched `played`, and left every phone visitor
    // arriving at a static, already-finished pack. The animation never played at all — the exact
    // opposite of the mobile design. It needs its own observer with a real threshold.
    const ioPlay = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => {
        if (!entry.isIntersecting || playArmed) return;
        playArmed = true;
        ioPlay.disconnect(); // one-shot: it only ever needs to answer "has she been seen?"
        if (mode === 'play' && !played) {
          playT0 = performance.now() - p * PLAY_MS;
          start();
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.35 },
    );
    ioPlay.observe(stage);

    // Live, not read-once: every other motion check in this repo responds to the OS setting
    // changing, and this one flips a whole render path.
    mqReduce.addEventListener('change', applyMode);
    mqDesktop.addEventListener('change', applyMode);
    applyMode();

    teardown = () => {
      stop();
      io.disconnect();
      ioPlay.disconnect();
      ro.disconnect();
      mqReduce.removeEventListener('change', applyMode);
      mqDesktop.removeEventListener('change', applyMode);
      delete stage.dataset.ftInit;
    };
  }

  initHeroStage();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', initHeroStage);
  document.addEventListener('astro:before-swap', (e) => {
    teardown?.();
    teardown = null;
    // Arm the INCOMING document before it paints. The is:inline arming script above cannot help
    // here: Astro runs scripts after the swap has already been painted, and it is deduped to
    // once per session anyway — so on a client-side nav back to /, this is the only thing
    // standing between the visitor and a finished-then-snap-backwards flash.
    (e as any).newDocument?.querySelector('[data-hero-stage]')?.setAttribute('data-ft-armed', '');
  });
</script>
